  <paragraph>So, what is 5 + 1 equal to in base 6? Well, start at the 5, and jump ahead one step. You land on 10.</paragraph>
  <figure>
    <image alt="A table with numbers 0 to 5, 10 to 15, 20 to 25, 30 to 35, 40 to 45, 50 to 55. An arrow points from 5 to 10"/>
  </figure>
  <figure>
    <image alt="A table with numbers 0 to 5, 10 to 15, 20 to 25, 30 to 35, 40 to 45, 50 to 55. A callout pointing to 10 reads, The 10 is one step past the 5."/>
  </figure>
  <paragraph>This means that, in base 6, 5 + 1 = 10.</paragraph>
  <paragraph>So, what is 5 + 2 in base 6? Well, 5 + 2 = 5 + 1 + 1, so 10 + 1…jump one more space and you land on 11. So, 5 + 2 = 11 in base 6.</paragraph>
  <figure>
    <image alt="A table with numbers 0 to 5, 10 to 15, 20 to 25, 30 to 35, 40 to 45, 50 to 55. Arrows point from 5 to 10 and from 10 to 11."/>
  </figure>
  <paragraph>And so it goes. Using that process, stepping one more along the list, we can fill in the remainder of the base 6 addition table (Table 4.4).</paragraph>
